  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Jelly-antler (Calocera viscosa) is a gelatinous fungus in the Dacrymycetaceae family that produces distinctive finger-like or branching fruiting bodies. Despite its plant-like appearance, it is actually a fungus that plays an important role in decomposing wood in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Gelatinous Texture",
      "description": "The fruiting bodies are translucent, jelly-like, and sticky to the touch, with a viscous consistency that distinguishes it from woody fungi.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Antler-like Shape",
      "description": "The fruiting bodies branch upward in a finger-like or antler-like pattern, typically orange-yellow to deep yellow in color.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and Growth",
      "description": "The structures are relatively small, usually 1-4 cm tall, and often form clusters on decaying wood.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Jelly-antler thrives on decaying hardwood logs and branches in moist, shaded forests throughout New England, particularly in areas with high humidity.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Calocera viscosa is a saprotroph that feeds on dead wood, and its gelatinous body can absorb and retain moisture, allowing it to remain active even during dry periods by rehydrating when moisture returns.",
